README.OSK -- mz 3.1 on OS-9/68000ORIG/ is mz as comp.sources.games posted it, volume 9 issue 87 (May1990): John Smith's maze game, "a pacman like game with intelligentmonsters" that always take the shortest path to you.  The shar carriestwo makefiles named Makefile and makefile; the second is kept here asORIG/makefile.2, since this file system cannot hold both names.The game is unchanged.  Everything the author called system dependentis in mzio.c, and all but one change is there, under #ifdef OSK.  Theone in mz.c: init_screen() puts the cursor at the start of each row ofthe room, which the posted code left to the terminal's wrap aftercolumn 80 -- true only in a window exactly 80 wide.  The changes inmzio.c:  headers   sys/time.h, sys/ioctl.h and fcntl.h are not included; the      sgtty calls come from "sgtty.h".  sgtty.h, sgtty.c  (new; vttest's, from SRC/vttest, unchanged)      gtty() and stty() on _gs_opt()/_ss_opt().  stty() turns echo off      and clears the interrupt, abort, end-of-file and pause characters      while the game runs, and puts every option back at the end.  get_time()  _sysdate(3,...) in place of gettimeofday(): seconds since      midnight, with the current tick in the low word and ticks per      second in the high word, turned into hundredths of a second as      before.  A game played across midnight will misjudge its timers,      as it would have under UNIX.      Once the game has started, curtime counts from one second rather      than zero.  main() starts every timer at curtime-1, which wraps to      the top of an unsigned int when curtime is 0 -- the room drawn      within one clock tick, which happens when the output is not paced      (a file, a pipe).  Then nothing moves, and ^C, which is read only      when the player moves, is never seen: the game runs for ever.  The      same wrap is in the posted code; a 1990 terminal drew too slowly      to meet it.  key_pressed()  _gs_rdy(0) and a one-byte read() in place of select().      Because sgtty.c clears the interrupt character, ^C arrives as a      byte; it is handed to request_quit(), which is what SIGINT did.  log_entry()  returns at once: the usage log was a file in the author's      own directory.mzmk.c and scrn, the author's maze-making aids, are carried as postedand not built.Keys: the numeric pad, 7 8 9 / 4 6 / 1 2 3; ^C quits.  The game needsTERM and a termcap entry with cm, ce and cl.MEASURED 2026-09-23 under os9exec: no instructions, skill level 1, theroom is drawn whole, the player moves on 6 and 2, the monster hunts it,and ^C returns to the shell with the final score line.  Left alone on aterminal, the monster takes all three lives and the game ends by itself.Before the curtime change, a run with its output in a file froze inabout half of all tries; after it, none in a dozen.